in afghanistan the taliban have  announced a caretaker cabinet for their  new government all the posts have been  filled by men mostly long-time members  of the hardline group the international  community had insisted on an inclusive  government otherwise financial aid to  afghanistan would be cut the u.s state  department says it's concerned by the  track records of some cabinet members  now in general it's the old guard of the  taliban regime that will be ruling the  country again  muller hassan akund will be the acting  prime minister he served as foreign  minister in the last uh the last time  the taliban were in power in the 1990s  like many of the incoming cab incoming  cabinet he is under un sanctions  abdul ghani baradar is a co-founder of  the taliban he will be deputy prime  minister he helped lead negotiations  with the u.s to withdraw troops from  afghanistan last year and prior to that  he spent eight years in prison in  pakistan  interior minister sir jaden harkani was  a fierce opponent of the u.s presence in  afghanistan now he's been linked to some  of the most violent terror attacks there  the fbi has issued a 10 million dollar  reward for information leading to his  capture now the taliban say this is an  acting government and deliberations are  ongoing to fill a number of posts that  are still vacant  and joining me now from kabul is  journalist ali latifi  ali the taliban promised a more moderate  rule but the uh interim government is  all male and made up of senior taliban  figures was there ever really a chance  it would be more  inclusive um based on their actions  there definitely was a chance because  they had spent much of the last three  weeks going around meeting with uh  different officials from the previous  government  you know  potentially making deals you know trying  to entice them so there was a hope that  they would somehow include or embrace  these people it's not to say that those  figures  weren't controversial in their own right  for different reasons but the hope was  that if those people could at least come  in  what it would do was um hopefully sort  of mitigate against some of the  taliban's more hard-line stances but if  you look at their current cabinet it's  all  you know old faces uh from their own  rule you know some people even have the  same job they had 25 years ago so  that you know they they hadn't they made  it seem like there was an opportunity  like there was some level of hope  for some kind of inclusion but that  hasn't panned out  so with this new government now in place  what are the taliban's most urgent  priorities  the most urgent priority is the economy  you know um you had said earlier that  the eu wanted to give a billion dollars  in aid to um or a billion euros in aid  to neighboring countries and other  places to keep refugees out  but you know that aid would be much  better spent in the avalon economy to  make sure that people can return to work  that they can access cash in banks  and that they can actually spend money  because the biggest issue right now is  that there is no cash in the in the  market and if you don't have cash you  can't spend money in afghanistan so the  taliban has to convince these countries  and these different financial  institutions these global financial  institutions to bring money back into  afghanistan because if they don't do  that then we're going to continue to see  people waiting hours and days in line at  banks hoping to take out as much money  as they can  while offices and other businesses  remain shuttered because they cannot  afford to pay anybody or they don't have  access to money or people don't have  access money to go out and and spend it  and you know shop and and eat out and  you know buy things um so it's very  important for them to get some kind of  financial  assurance from the rest of the world  okay ali latifi reporting for us from  kabul thanks so much  now as the rush to escape the taliban  intensifies the european union wants to  avoid a repeat of the 2015 migration  crisis when more than a million people  arrived in europe to do so the bloc has  pledged to provide between 600 million  and 1 billion euros now that money will  support charities afghanistan's  neighbors and transit countries to  provide food and shelter to refugees  fleeing the taliban the aim according to  eu leaders is to prevent the recurrence  of uncontrolled large-scale illegal  migration movements faced in the past  and we can speak now to lars  castellucci of the social democrat party  and a member of the german bundestag mr  castellucci thanks so much for joining  us today  how prepared are germany and the eu for  hundreds of thousands more refugees  um  i don't agree that there will be  hundreds of thousands of refugees  so far  the main thing and the most important  thing we have to manage right now is to  at all  get open borders for those who want to  leave the country and this is part of  the negotiations with the taliban  government  but of course many migrants are already  waiting at eu borders wanting to come  what is germany doing to help ease the  situation  well this is again a completely  different question on the eu borders we  need a common  european asylum system that is working  and functioning and this should be based  on solidarity but also on the values  that uh connect us in europe and uh we  have to  um  improve  all the administration regarding  refugees and  distinguish better and faster between  those who  apply for  protection internet international  protection and the others and then  distribute those who  successfully apply for international  protection into the countries in europe  that  allow them to to enter  so obviously improvements need to be  made and yet since 2015 when more than a  million people arrived in europe there's  really been no revised plan created  since then to handle a new refugee  movement why not  because europe is not  in a situation that all the countries  have the common goals and that's a  really deep problem but i don't like the  crisis talk  all in all the situation can be handled  and it can also be handled if countries  a number of countries in europe um take  charge and uh work together and i think  that is what has to be done now  we can't wait for for countries that are  not uh willing to join us in our common  efforts  okay now now turkey became the home of  one of the world's largest migrant  populations back in 2015 but ankara is  now saying it will take no more refugees  what impact would you say that will have  yeah i do understand turkey because they  took over  more migrants than the whole of europe  altogether  and of course we supported them with  billions that went into the country  to build schools to build hospitals and  to support directly  the refugees that stranded in turkey  but we have to negotiate how we can  share the burden on a solidarity  in a solidarity way and that's the  that's the kind of thing that was also  needed before the afghan crisis exploded  in the way that we saw the last couple  of weeks okay lars castellucci of the  social democrats in germany thanks so  much for your time today  thank you  now many afghans believe there is no  safe future for them except outside  afghanistan dw's nick conley met one  family who fled to neighbouring  tajikistan to protect their four  daughters  as an afghan government official  muhammad ahmadi had enough reasons to be  aware of the taliban  but it's his four daughters  two university students and two of  school age and fears for their future  that convince him to leave his country  he tells us  the taliban sent me threats  one of the things they said was that i  should not let my daughters marry  non-believers  instead they said my daughters should  marry young taliban fighters  the decision to leave wasn't taken  lightly the family's eldest too tamana  and kreshma were just months away from  graduating in political science when the  family left this summer  a university education that these young  women had clung to in spite of the odds  continuing with their studies even after  surviving an attack on kabul university  by the so-called islamic state last  november  they had guns and they just came to our  class and started firing at it everyone  they even threw a grenade and  a lot of people was  injured and died there  we escaped from the window but a lot of  our other classmates who couldn't escape  they died unfortunately  we studied together four years three and  a half year and then i just  go after a week there and there's no one  there  taman and kreshma weren't just sisters  and classmates they were also rivals for  the top spot in their degree program  before we met this attack happened in  kabul university the three or  two and a half year of our university  was i could say the best years of our  life that we are studying we were  studying and we were working  on our dreams but after this attack it  was all vanished we was  more like scared  and then hopeful for that aflamestone in  that day  as it later turned out freshman  tamanna's parents had read the signs  correctly  leaving while it was still possible to  board a commercial flight for safety  into jigsaw  most of their classmates have been less  fortunate  a lot of demons have gone on offline  since they have cons since taliban  overtake afghanistan we can find out  where they are actually  their parents who lived through the  taliban's first period in control back  in the 1990s now see history repeating  itself  at a recent demonstration in kabul the  taliban beat the demonstrators until  they bled used tear gas the taliban  still don't respect women  samia spent 30 years teaching in a mixed  sex primary school  her colleagues back in kabul tell her  that this year the girls have not  returned for lessons after the holidays  their parents are just too scared  if it's clear that there's no going back  to afghanistan it's equally clear that  tajikistan is only a temporary stop on  the way to a new life somewhere else  refugees are essentially unable to study  or work in the country which itself sees  hundreds of thousands of locals leave in  search of work abroad every year  all this family can do now is wait and  hope  hope that one of their applications for  resettlement is finally approved
 

